The executable also carries other people's code, all of it under permissive
licenses (MIT, two- and three-clause BSD, ISC, Apache-2.0). Their copyright
notices and license texts are in licenses/, one directory per module, indexed by
licenses/README.md. That directory is generated by cmd/pat-licenses from the
packages actually linked into the binary, so it follows the dependencies instead
of being a snapshot of whenever someone last looked.

Some things do not come from a Go module and live in licenses/manually-added/,
each with its reason written next to it:

  - libopus (Xiph.Org Foundation and others, BSD-3-Clause), compiled to
    WebAssembly and embedded in the executable by internal/opuswasm;

  - the WebAssembly bridge around it (Jiang Yiheng and the Go Opus Authors,
    MIT): about two hundred lines of C, from github.com/jj11hh/opus, compiled
    into the same artefact. We no longer depend on that module, but the code it
    compiled still ships;

  - the Go standard library (Google LLC, BSD-3-Clause, with the patent grant in
    PATENTS), statically linked;

  - Tabler Icons (Paweł Kuna, MIT), four glyphs pasted into the viewer page.

The web pages served by the monitor load nothing from outside: no fonts, no
libraries, no remote stylesheets. Everything the browser receives is in this
repository.
